i look back at how kovit 19 impacted  southeast asia this year and left not  just death but also hope in its wake  in mid-july indonesia experienced the  kind of devastation from covet 19 that  india had just a few weeks before the  delta variant tore through the country  making it the epicenter of the pandemic  in asia at the time indonesia had fully  vaccinated less than six percent of its  population as a result one in three  people were testing positive with many  ending up in hospitals  dw went behind the scenes at one such  facility to see firsthand how healthcare  workers struggled to keep the system  from complete collapse  getting into her coved protective gear  takes some time and more than a little  courage  but nurse desanti is just glad to have  equipment available  after graduating from nursing school  last year she was sent to the front line  of the pandemic in jakarta  and became infected herself  a few months after she recovered the  second wave hit the country  [Music]  we even had to rotate the oxygen bottles  for patients in the emergency room  and they kept coming and coming even  after we were out of beds and oxygen  those who came had low oxygen levels i  was overwhelmed  officially kovid has claimed more than  100 000 lives in indonesia with many  health workers among the victims  they've mostly been given the chinese  cinevac vaccine  now medical staff like dr puspita are  getting a booster shot of moderna to  better protect them  i'm really tired  this is just me but when there are a lot  of cases i start talking to myself  this is insane i have to work with covet  19 cases over and over i often feel like  crying but then i realize i'm not alone  my colleagues are also working hard  but by october indonesia had passed the  peak of infections but people were still  dying many alone in their homes it fell  upon volunteers to step in and organize  funerals for these victims of covet 19  if only to give them some peace in death  antonio sayano puts his life on the line  caring for the dead the 53 year old is a  member of a so-called quick burial team  one of the many kovet 19 volunteer  groups in the city of jakarta their  motto  is people of joho district we are  responsive agile and tough  the volunteers help overwhelmed cemetery  officials to deal with the many covet 19  victims in indonesia despite much lower  case numbers in recent weeks their help  in this part of java is still needed  i am suffering from all the heat  and all the sweat  [Music]  the funerals process is entirely in our  hands because the others are afraid  it's not just that some people are  afraid many gravediggers are simply  exhausted and can no longer for instance  collect bodies from people's homes  during the peak of the pandemic  graveyards were open around the clock  in july alone  570 people died here more than in the  previous 12th month  normally hayono works as a cleaner but  in the pandemic people like him have  prevented the entire system from  collapsing his family is proud that he  volunteers  but they also fear he might get infected  the volunteers hope that the fast burial  of bodies will not only bring down the  infection risk enhance the mortality  rate but also help to reduce the  psychological burden for morning  families  hyonu is a christian and says he regards  helping as a duty  yes  we're actually a little afraid  but we throw that fear away and put our  hope in the almighty  it's great to help people in need  and make sure things run smoothly and  safely  and with all the precautions in place  haryana is positive that he can achieve  this goal at the end of the day  with a negative result  in myanmar meanwhile a double crisis  pushed the country to the brink this  summer political instability after the  military coup and a brutal second wave  of covet 19. thousands of healthcare  workers had walked out in protest  against the junta the few hospitals that  stayed open couldn't keep up with the  strain many people turned to religious  groups for oxygen to treat the sick  it's just after dawn in yangon myanmar's  largest city  in front of this mosque the line for  empty oxygen tanks slowly shuffles  forward  people have already been waiting for  hours desperate for a refill of medical  oxygen for loved ones with covid19  if we want to get medical oxygen we have  to come here early even though i came  here at around four in the morning i had  to stand back in the line as some people  had already arrived  for many in myanmar treating kovid 19 is  a do it yourself project  oxygen and hospital beds are scarce  the mosque decided to offer oxygen  as a way to help  initially we thought of distributing  meals to covid quarantine centers like  we did last year  but we changed our decision as we saw  people in desperate need of medical  oxygen  it's available here free of charge  religion ethnic background doesn't  matter  all who wait are served it's a lifeline  as most people here can't afford to pay  prices have more than tripled with the  infection surge  the military government has not been  able to effectively manage the health  crisis  authorities are now even forcing oxygen  factories to close so we're struggling  to get enough supplies to refill the  oxygen tanks  at public hospitals there aren't enough  medical facilities and they're finding  it hard to cope with the demand for  oxygen and other essential drugs and  equipment  with the country in crisis these muslim  volunteers say they want to join with  the buddhist majority in the fight  against covert 19  and many in need are taking up the offer  since they have nowhere else to turn  in malaysia the pain of loss was still  fresh during august the month of the  hungry ghost festival buddhist and  taoist families traditionally take this  time to honor the dead with gifts and  one religious shop owner came up with an  item that many had wished to give  departed loved ones  mr leong makes the jab many departed  loved ones never got  covid19 vaccines  except these doses are made from paper  devout families are buying them up as  gifts to appease the spirits of the dead  the kovid 19 situation in malaysia is  quite serious  many people died before receiving the  vaccine  therefore i hope with this product  it can help the deceased to fulfill  their dying wish  [Music]  the heartache over vaccines is high this  year  thousands in malaysia have died before  getting the chance to get the jab  mr leong can barely keep up with demand  for his paper sets  things  families who bought the paper vaccines  will in the end burn them as offerings  during the hungry ghost festival  when spirits are thought to return to  earth  though this vaccine couldn't prevent the  death of loved ones  many hope the gesture will at least  bring them peace in the afterlife  another place where kovat19 has taken a  heavy toll is the thai capital bangkok  among the many who died were some of the  owners of the city's well-known street  food stalls and as we report some of  their children are stepping up to the  plate to make sure their businesses  continue to thrive  it is now one of the first things out of  which  does when setting up his food stall  alongside the michelin stars he places a  photo of his father who died this year  of covert 19.  he ran the stall for nearly 50 years  turning it into a must visit of the  bangkok street food scene  for adewitch there was no doubt he would  continue serving up the stall's renowned  rolled rice noodles  this shop was what my father loved the  most  and i love him the most  i have to keep it going  no matter what  i'm like before  stalls like this one in bangkok's  chinatown were under threat even before  the coronavirus pandemic facing  competition from upmarket restaurants  and resistance from city planners  wanting to clean up the crowded  pavements  but some say you can't beat these  gastronomic gems  handed down from generation to  generation  this noodle stall has existed for so  long since when my mother and my aunt  were young people know that it is famous  when they come to chinatown they know  that they have to come and eat at the  noodle stall under the old cinema  building  it's why sarisa saitan keeps on going  her mother was another well-known street  food vendor who died of covid  theresa's had to learn quickly how to  make her mother's favorite duck stew  i'll be overjoyed if customers say our  duck still tastes like my mother's  it's given me some encouragement  some tell me not to quit because they  can't find food like this anywhere else  and so in this corner of bangkok street  food is now more than just about its  taste  in the time of covid  it's about keeping memories alive
 

